<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Plans – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Time Capsule</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Am Preparing for the Next Five Years</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Write simple, informal emails/letters. Interactive: Participate in a written discussion on paper, through apps, online, or in a forum discussion.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write a letter to their future self, describing detailed career goals and the steps needed to achieve them (e.g., "In five years, I will be pursuing a degree in marine biology at the University of Panama, supported by an internship at a conservation organization.").
            Can participate in a written discussion about time capsule items (e.g., "The teacher said that we should include photos of our class in the project.").
            Can write a brief note explaining a personal item for the time capsule (e.g., "I have chosen this book because it will help me grow.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates students' prior knowledge and connects to the theme through engaging activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a visual of a time capsule and asks students to quickly brainstorm and write down 3-5 items they would put in a time capsule for their future self, and one career goal they hope to achieve. (e.g., "What would you like to achieve in five years?"). Students write individually on small slips of paper. Emphasize using new vocabulary like 'career', 'goals', 'future', 'possibilities'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models by writing an example item and a goal on the board: "I'd include a photo of my current classroom because I want to remember my students. In five years, I hope I will be developing new curriculum materials." The teacher highlights how to use future continuous for ongoing plans. Pronunciation of /iː/ in "achieve" and stress in "problem-solving" is subtly modeled when reading the example aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you need to write full sentences for your goals, or just words?" (Expected answer: Full sentences.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">New language input connected to the scenario and theme is presented, along with comprehension tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with a short, informal email written by a high school student to their future self, to be opened in five years. The email describes their current career aspirations, the steps they plan to take, and includes reflections on items they might include in a time capsule. (e.g., "Subject: Letter to my 2029 Self!"). It includes modals for possibilities (e.g., "I might pursue a career in technology"), future continuous (e.g., "I will be studying software development"), and reported speech (e.g., "My counselor said that critical thinking skills are essential for future jobs."). The text should incorporate vocabulary like 'education', 'internship', 'experience', 'skills', 'leadership', 'high-demand', 'opportunities'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret and analyze short, informal written texts about personal future goals and time capsule items, identifying key information and linguistic features.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the email individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this email?" (Expected answer: To tell their future self about current plans and hopes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students answer T/F questions based on the text (e.g., "The student definitely knows they will become a doctor." - False; "The student plans to gain experience through an internship." - True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the future career possibility (e.g., 'technology') with a supporting action (e.g., 'researching online courses').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on accuracy through controlled and semi-controlled writing activities, targeting specific grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using modals for career possibilities ('might pursue', 'could develop'), future continuous ('will be working', 'will be studying'), and reported speech ('My teacher said that I should consider...', 'My parents advised me to...'). Emphasis on target vocabulary: 'career', 'education', 'training', 'internship', 'skills', 'leadership', 'problem-solving', 'critical thinking', 'opportunities'. The teacher also reminds students to be mindful of the pronunciation of words like "achieve" and "volunteer" as they read their work silently or aloud for peer checking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze & Sentence Building):</b> Students complete sentences with appropriate modals or future continuous forms, and select suitable vocabulary. Example: "In five years, I _______ (study) medicine, but I _______ (also/explore) engineering. My mentor told me that I _______ (need) to develop strong _______ (critical thinking) skills."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students transform direct speech about advice into reported speech related to career planning. Example: "My teacher said, 'You should research different university programs.'" becomes "My teacher said that I should research different university programs." Students also rephrase statements about future plans using different modals or the future continuous (e.g., "I plan to work as a software developer" -> "I will be working as a software developer").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Paragraph Writing (Pair Check):</b> In pairs, students write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a classmate's hypothetical future goal and the steps they might take, based on a sentence starter provided by the teacher (e.g., "Maria mentioned she might pursue a career in marine biology..."). They must include at least one modal, one future continuous verb, and one reported speech statement. Pairs then exchange paragraphs and check for correct grammar and vocabulary use. This activity encourages writing about peers' future goals, integrating the pragmatic function of asking peers about future goals.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative writing task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual writing task: Students write a letter to their future self, to be placed in a class time capsule. Alternatively, they write a brief note (50-80 words) to accompany a personal item they would hypothetically include in a time capsule, explaining its significance and connection to their future aspirations. The teacher provides a template for the letter/note.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task description:</b> Students write a letter (minimum 100 words) to their future self (to be opened in five years). The letter should describe their detailed career goals and the steps they are currently planning to achieve them. They must include at least two examples of modals for possibilities, two examples of future continuous, and one piece of advice or information shared through reported speech. They should use a minimum of five vocabulary words from the target list (e.g., 'career', 'education', 'internship', 'skills', 'opportunities'). Students should also explain one item they would put in the time capsule and why it is relevant to their future, demonstrating the pragmatic function of explaining item choices (e.g., "I am including a photo of my robotics project because it represents my goal to become an electronic engineer in the future."). Encourage students to reflect on 'leadership', 'problem-solving', and 'critical thinking' as desirable skills. As students write, the teacher circulates, offering support and ensuring the use of target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment of student writing and an opportunity for self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher collects the letters/notes and uses a simple rubric to assess the integration of target grammar, vocabulary, and task completion. Focus on clarity of ideas and the effective use of the target language. Additionally, a quick peer review can be implemented where students read a classmate's letter and provide one positive comment and one suggestion for improvement regarding clarity or grammar use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written letters to their future selves or their time capsule item notes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of career goals and steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for possibilities (e.g., 'might pursue', 'will be working').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of future continuous for ongoing plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate integration of reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'career', 'internship', 'skills', 'opportunities').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explanation of time capsule item relevance.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I used successfully in my writing today was _________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it challenging/easy to use _________ (modals/future continuous/reported speech) in my letter."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages self-reflection for students and the teacher, and links to the subsequent lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about your future plans did you enjoy the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific skill or piece of advice you will remember as you work towards your future goals?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully integrated modals and future continuous into their letters, demonstrating a good grasp of expressing possibilities and ongoing plans. Some students found using reported speech slightly more challenging, especially with tense shifts. The vocabulary related to career goals (e.g., 'internship', 'critical thinking', 'leadership') was well-utilized. The peer-checking activity provided valuable immediate feedback. For next time, I could provide more specific sentence frames for reported speech to reinforce accuracy. The pronunciation of 'achieve' and 'volunteer' can be reinforced in the next lesson through focused reading aloud of their written pieces if applicable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students consolidating and summarizing the information from their letters and time capsule notes into a collective class time capsule project. They will mediate information about their peers' future plans and present the class's collective aspirations, integrating all four skills and 21st-century competencies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>